The property is called **`$jsonviews`**. It contains `version: 1`, **`views`**, and **`schema`**.

**`views`** describes where to look and how to show it: a named page, a table with chosen columns, or a board grouped by status. **`schema`** describes fields by path: a date, a checkbox, a choice, or the body of a page.

```json
{
  "$jsonviews": {
    "version": 1,
    "schema": { "$.note.body": { "type": "body" } },
    "views": [{ "name": "Note", "path": "$.note" }]
  },
  "note": { "title": "Hello", "body": "A page inside a file." }
}
```

Without `$jsonviews`, the app infers useful field types and views from the data. You can then make those choices explicit. Invalid settings are shown as issues rather than silently replaced. In the current format, the view list is named `views`, not `use`.
